  "verdict": "the two nodes are not equally usable and the difference is the whole point. A tank node is disqualified: 10 pF of probe moves the centre by 1.49 MHz, the bandwidth by 22 % and the 29 MHz relative suppression by +17 dB, so the measurement would destroy the thing it measures. The buffered rf node is conditionally usable: the same 10 pF probe pulls it 6.81 dB down at 31 MHz, but because that is a smooth 1 kOhm||C pole the bandwidth error is only +1.5 % and the 29-vs-31 MHz relative error is 0.45 dB. So shape and relative suppression survive at the buffered node; ABSOLUTE amplitude does not, and must be corrected or reported as a relative quantity only.",
  "absolute_vs_relative": "do not quote a measured Vpp or gain in dB from a passive probe at the buffered node without stating the droop. With a 10 pF probe the honest number at 31 MHz is 6.81 dB below the true value; with a 3 pF probe it is 1.28 dB.",
  "what_changes_the_answer": [
    "an active probe at 1-3 pF: droop falls to 0.16-1.28 dB and the relative error to 0.02-0.14 dB, which makes the absolute reading defensible too",
    "a dedicated on-board test buffer with a 50 Ohm output driven into a 50 Ohm scope input; this is a measurement fixture and must be documented as such, never counted as part of the receiver",
    "a spectrum analyser or VNA with a high-impedance probe, which avoids the problem but is equipment the project does not have"
  ],
  "honest_note": "this replaces an earlier draft conclusion in this same script that claimed the buffered node was safe. The computed pole shows it is not; the claim was withdrawn before it reached the report."
}